---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import BlurFaceInPhoto from '@/components/tools/image-edit/BlurFaceInPhoto.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="blur-face-in-photo" width="wide">
  <BlurFaceInPhoto client:load defaultEffect="blur" defaultShape="ellipse" subject="face" />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to blur faces in a photo</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Drop one or more photos on the page. Each photo is checked for faces, and every face gets a box.</li>
      <li>Click a box to keep that face visible. Click it again to hide it. The list under the photo shows each face with a small picture.</li>
      <li>Drag on the photo to add a box over a face or anything else the model did not mark.</li>
      <li>Choose blur, pixelate or a solid fill, an oval or a rectangle, and the strength.</li>
      <li>Download the photo, or all photos as one ZIP file.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      On a phone, pinch to zoom and move the photo with two fingers. With a keyboard, press Tab to go to a box, Enter to
      switch it, the arrow keys to move it, + and - to resize it and Delete to remove it.
    </p>

    <h2>The face model</h2>
    <p>
      The faces are found by a small face detection model (230 kB, MIT license). It runs in your browser in a model
      runtime of 15 MB. Both files come from this site the first time you use the tool and are kept in your browser, so
      the tool also works offline after that.
    </p>
    <p>
      The model looks at the whole photo at three sizes and then at overlapping tiles of the full image. This finds large
      faces in a portrait and faces of about 10 pixels in a crowd photo. A 12-megapixel group photo takes a few seconds on
      a laptop.
    </p>

    <h2>How to check that nothing is uploaded</h2>
    <p>
      Open the developer tools of your browser (F12), select the Network tab and drop a photo. You see the model files
      load from this site the first time and no request with your photo. You can also load the tool once, turn off your
      network and use it.
    </p>

    <h2>Blur, pixelate or fill</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Style</th><th>Use it for</th><th>Can it be undone?</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Blur</td><td>Faces, tattoos, house numbers</td><td>No, at 50% strength or more the detail is gone.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Pixelate</td><td>Faces, when the effect should look deliberate</td><td>Not for faces. Short text can sometimes be guessed.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Fill</td><td>Text: names, plates, card numbers</td><td>No. The area is one colour.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      Strength is measured against the size of each box, so a small face gets the same effect as a large one. The preview
      shows the same result as the saved file.
    </p>

    <h2>The metadata in the file</h2>
    <p>
      A phone photo often holds GPS coordinates, the time, the camera model and a small preview image of the photo. The
      saved file is written again from the pixels, so none of this is in it. The preview image matters: other editors
      often keep it, and it still shows the faces. To see what a photo holds, open it in the
      <a href="/image-metadata-viewer">image metadata viewer</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Limits</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Faces seen from the side, from behind, under a mask or very blurred can be missed. Check every photo and add boxes where needed.</li>
      <li>Some boxes mark things that are not faces, such as hands or patterns. Click them to keep them visible.</li>
      <li>Reflections, photos on a wall and screens can show a face again. Look for them.</li>
      <li>Very large photos use a lot of memory. On a phone, photos above about 50 megapixels can fail.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Related tools</h2>
    <p>
      <a href="/blur-faces-in-video">Video Face Blur</a> does the same for a video,
      <a href="/face-blur-camera">Face Blur Camera</a> blurs faces on your webcam live, and
      <a href="/blur-license-plate">Blur License Plate</a> finds number plates. To clear metadata without other changes,
      use <a href="/remove-exif-data">Remove EXIF Data</a>.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
